PROJECT GOAL: We aim to verify if the user needs to set up a build environment to resolve the issue:

1. Does not need build environment
   (The solutions/answers can be validated without build environment, e.g., it's a conceptual issue, documentation issue, or can be verified with simple code analysis)

2. Can be dockerized without any issue
   (The solutions must require a build environment to be verified)

3. Requires build environment but hard to be dockerized or cannot be reproduced now
   (The solution must require a build environment, but it is difficult to containerize for reasons such as: hardware dependencies, network dependencies, external API access with authentication, timing-dependent behaviors, requiring MacOS, multiple users interacting simultaneously, licensed software, etc.)

IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:
- Choose category 1 whenever possible. If the LLM can analyze the issue and verify the solution without needing to build or run the code, always select category 1.
- Only classify an issue as requiring a build environment (categories 2 or 3) when it's ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY to actually build and run the code to verify if the solution works.

GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2015-11-13T14:20:11Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Why does the scan operator only emit the initialValue when receiving the first value from the observable (or on completion)?
Description: Hello, I am wondering why the scan() method with an initialValue only emits the initialValue when the first value from the observable arrives. I had expected this to happen instantly upon subscription. 

For now, I worked around this issue by NOT providing an initialValue, and adding ".startWith()" to the observable I am scanning. 

In the `OperatorScan` class:

``` java

        return new Subscriber<T>(child) {
            ...

            @Override
            public void onNext(T currentValue) {
                emitInitialValueIfNeeded(child);
                ...
                child.onNext(this.value);
            }

            @Override
            public void onError(Throwable e) {
                child.onError(e);
            }

            @Override
            public void onCompleted() {
                emitInitialValueIfNeeded(child);
                child.onCompleted();
            }
        }
```

Example scenario: scan an observable that counts from 0 to 4, emitting a number every second.

``` java
Observable
    .create(new Observable.OnSubscribe<Integer>() {
        @Override
        public void call(Subscriber<? super Integer> subscriber) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(1000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    subscriber.onError(e);
                }
                subscriber.onNext(i);
            }
            subscriber.onCompleted();
        }
    })
    .scan(0, new Func2<Integer, Integer, Integer>() {
        @Override
        public Integer call(Integer sum, Integer nextNumber) {
            return sum + nextNumber;
        }
    })
```

The timeline of this observable would be:

0ms : subscribe()
1000ms: 0 <- the initial value
1001ms: 0 <- the acummulated result of 0 + the initial value
2000ms: 1 <- the accumulated result of 1 + 0
3000ms: 3 <- the accumulated result of 2 + 1
4000ms: 6 <- the accumulated result of 3 + 2
5000ms: 10 <- the accumulated result of 4 + 6

My question is: why does this happen lazily. Why not immediately call `child.onNext(initialValue)` when the subscription starts? Why does the given example not produce value 0 at 1 ms? 

Thank you in advance.


